This empty file tells GitHub Pages to serve the repository as plain static
files instead of running it through Jekyll first.

Two reasons it is here.

Jekyll hides anything whose name begins with an underscore, and silently
rewrites files it thinks are templates. Nothing in app/ starts with an
underscore today, but a future data file might, and the failure would look
like a missing file rather than a policy.

It also matches how the map is actually deployed on the Pocket Project's
server: files handed to a web server exactly as they sit on disk, no build
step in between. Turning Jekyll off keeps GitHub Pages an honest rehearsal
for that rather than a slightly different environment.
